  "rationale": "Haiku's blanket move of all 98 topics to formal-systems is overbroad. The course spans material from introductory automata theory through graduate-level complexity theory -- a single stage cannot fit all of it. SPLIT rationale: (1) The automata/formal languages portion (DFA, NFA, regex, CFG, PDA, LBA) plus core computability (Turing machines, halting problem, decidability, basic reductions) plus standard NP-completeness (P, NP, Cook-Levin, classic NP-complete problems) constitute the standard single-semester Theory of Computation course (Sipser chapters 1-7). This material IS a required CS course at the 300 level, right at the formal-systems/advanced boundary. At many universities, automata theory alone is a 200-level course. These 47 topics fit formal-systems as the upper range of 'standard introductory and intermediate undergraduate curriculum (100-300 level).' (2) The remaining 51 topics -- circuit complexity, interactive proofs, PCP theorem, descriptive complexity, alternating TMs, randomized complexity classes, parameterized complexity, Kolmogorov complexity, arithmetical hierarchy, Turing degrees, Rice's theorem, space hierarchy theorems -- go well beyond what a typical undergraduate encounters in a single ToC course. These are advanced topics (many graduate-level) that require deep proof maturity and belong squarely in the 'upper-division specialty (300-400 level)' or beyond. Moving them to formal-systems would place interactive proofs and the PCP theorem alongside introductory chemistry and cell biology, which is absurd.",
